@startuml Circuit_Breaker_State_Machine
title Circuit Breaker — Generic State Machine
' Applies to all 7 circuit breaker instances (CB-FRAUD, CB-NOTIFY, CB-DB-PRIMARY,
' CB-DB-REPLICA, CB-CACHE, CB-EXCHANGE, CB-SETTLEMENT).
' Per-instance thresholds, reset timeouts, and fallback behaviors are in
' docs/09-fault-tolerance.md — the state machine structure itself is identical
' across all of them; only the numbers differ.

[*] --> CLOSED

state CLOSED {
  CLOSED : Requests pass through to the real dependency
  CLOSED : Failures counted in a rolling time window
}

state OPEN {
  OPEN : Requests fail immediately — no call attempted
  OPEN : The dependency's fallback behavior executes instead
}

state HALF_OPEN {
  HALF_OPEN : A small number of probe requests are allowed through
  HALF_OPEN : Real calls attempted; results determine the next transition
}

CLOSED --> OPEN : failure count reaches\nthreshold within the window
OPEN --> HALF_OPEN : reset timeout elapses
HALF_OPEN --> CLOSED : probe request(s) succeed
HALF_OPEN --> OPEN : probe request fails

note right of OPEN
  While OPEN, callers never wait on the
  real dependency's timeout — they get
  the fallback response immediately.
  This is what prevents the thread-pool
  exhaustion seen in bottleneck BN-003.
end note

note right of HALF_OPEN
  Only a limited number of probes are
  allowed through at once, so a still-
  struggling dependency isn't immediately
  hit with full traffic the moment the
  reset timeout expires.
end note

@enduml
